==English==
{{wikipedia|ZIP Code}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|zipcode|ZIP Code|ZIP code|Zip Code|Zip code}}

===Etymology===
From {{m|en|ZIP Code}}, a trademark of the {{w|United States Postal Service}}, from {{m|en|ZIP|t={{w|Zone Improvement Plan}}}}, introduced 1963.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈzɪp koʊd/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Qwertygiy-zip code.wav|a=General American}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|US}} A [[postal code]], especially for addresses served by the US Postal Service, consisting of a five- or nine-figure number.
#: {{syn|en|zip|ZIP}}
#: {{hyper|en|postal code|postcode}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Judy Root Aulette; Judith G. Wittner; Kristin Blakely|title=Gendered Worlds|year=2009|publisher=Oxford University Press|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=Qo0UAQAAIAAJ&q=%22zip+code%22+90210&dq=%22zip+code%22+90210&hl=&cd=30&source=gbs_api|isbn=978-0-19-537111-6|page=351|passage=''90210'' was a television show popular in the 1990s about a group of high school students and their families who all lived in the '''zip code''' 90210 in Beverly Hills, California, an affluent suburb of Los Angeles.}}
# {{lb|en|US|by extension}} Any [[postal code]].
#: {{syn|en|postal code|postcode}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Alan Simpson; Paul Lichtman|title=The First Book of Lotus 1-2-3|year=1990|publisher=H.W. Sams & Company|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=RzMEMYV0XNQC&q=%22foreign+zip+code%22&dq=%22foreign+zip+code%22&hl=en&cd=10&source=gbs_api|isbn=978-0-672-27301-8|passage=For example, the foreign '''zip code''' H24 JZL is most certainly not a number.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Jaime Reed|title=Burning Emerald|year=2011|publisher=Kensington Publishing Corp.|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=mwt8FDpQQEYC&pg=PA46&dq=%22european+zip+code%22&hl=en&cd=2&source=gbs_api#v=onepage&q=%22european%20zip%20code%22&f=false|isbn=978-0-7582-8010-7|page=46|passage=Carrying the package to the stairs inside, I noticed it was addressed to Mom and  me with a European '''zip code''' and stamp.}}
# {{lb|en|Philippines}} A [[postal code]], especially for addresses served by {{w|PhilPost}}, consisting of a four-figure number.
# {{lb|en|sports|by extension}} A grouping of athletes or teams with similar skill level.
#: {{ux|en|Wallace and Brewer are almost certain to finish 1-2 in some order – no one else is even in the same '''zip code'''.}}

